{
  "Comment": "Table-builder control plane. TWO branches over ONE spine: validate-authorize → {check-table-absent | load-table-definition} → inspect-source → infer-schema → {execute-ddl | diff-schema → apply-refresh} → RecordSuccess → Succeeded. inspect-source and infer-schema are SHARED by both and know nothing about mode — load-table-definition normalises the state into exactly what a create would have handed them (rewritten prefix, reconstructed declared partition keys), which is why a refresh needed a second entry verb rather than a flag. Each Task threads the running request object via Payload.$=$ + OutputPath=$.Payload. Transient errors are retried on every verb: Lambda service faults, plus each verb's own throttleable AWS call mapped in-Lambda to the TransientError these Retry lists name — validate-authorize's registry read, check-table-absent's Glue GetTable, inspect-source's S3 listing, infer-schema's S3 head probe, execute-ddl's Athena polls. Any terminal verb failure is caught and routed to the shared recorded-failure state, which shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, database, tableName, status:'failed', reason } the notification producer reads; RecordSuccess shapes the success twin, whose `status` is THREADED from the verb that produced it ('created' | 'unchanged' | 'refreshed'). A partitioned table needs NO MSCK REPAIR (projection) — the single execute-ddl CREATE is terminal for both flat and partitioned sources.",
  "TimeoutSeconds": 3600,
  "StartAt": "ValidateAuthorize",
  "States": {
    "ValidateAuthorize": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "First step. The Pipe forwards the raw SQS batch (a JSON ARRAY), so Payload.$=$ hands the array to the validator (it reads record[0]); OutputPath=$.Payload makes the validated request object the whole state for the next steps. A failure here REPLACES the state rather than merging into it, so its Catch (no ResultPath — the input is an array, not an object) routes to ValidateFailureSeed, which seeds null identity fields so the shared recorded-failure state can reference them uniformly.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${validate_authorize_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "Next": "ValidateFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteByMode"
    },
    "RouteByMode": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "A refresh is a re-run of inspect-source + infer-schema against a table that already EXISTS, so it takes the mirror image of check-table-absent: load-table-definition, which reads the live Glue table, decides whether it is refreshable, and rewrites space.prefix to the table's own location so the two shared verbs need no mode branch. THE IsPresent CONJUNCT IS NOT DECORATION: a Choice rule whose Variable path is absent raises States.Runtime, which no Catch anywhere can catch, so a bare StringEquals would KILL an execution rather than fall through to Default on the very input Default exists for — an envelope written before this spec, or one in flight while the two deploy workflows land out of order.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "StringEquals": "refresh" }
          ],
          "Next": "StampExecution"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "CheckTableAbsent"
    },
    "StampExecution": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Puts the EXECUTION ARN on the state so the lock's holder identifies the execution rather than the request. The distinction is the whole point: requestId is a property of the MESSAGE, and Pipes delivery is at-least-once, so two executions of one redelivered message would carry the same request id — and the re-entrancy branch in table_lock (which exists for an SFN Task retry, where the ARN is genuinely the same) would then let both hold the lock, silently and with no refusal event. A Pass is used rather than the Task's own Parameters because reading $$ there means building an object, which WRAPS the payload — and every notification path in this plane reads the request at the TOP level of the event. ResultPath adds a key and disturbs nothing.",
      "Parameters": {
        "arn.$": "$$.Execution.Id"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.execution",
      "Next": "AcquireRefreshLock"
    },
    "AcquireRefreshLock": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Takes the per-table lock BEFORE load-table-definition, so it spans the whole read-modify-write rather than only the write — the point being that run A's GetTable and run A's UpdateTable are not separated by run B's UpdateTable. It sits AFTER ValidateAuthorize for the other half of the reason: an unauthorized request must not be able to touch another user's lock at all. A refusal is TerminalError refresh_already_running, which the decorator notifies and this Catch records; refresh_already_running appears in NO Retry list, because a held lock is an answer and not a transient fault. The holder written into the lock is the EXECUTION ARN, stamped onto the state by StampExecution — see that state's comment for why a Pass rather than this task's own Parameters.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${acquire_refresh_lock_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "LoadTableDefinition"
    },
    "LoadTableDefinition": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${load_table_definition_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "CheckTableAbsent": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${check_table_absent_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "InspectSource": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${inspect_source_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InferSchema"
    },
    "InferSchema": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${infer_schema_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteTailByMode"
    },
    "RouteTailByMode": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "The two branches share everything up to here and diverge on what they do with the inferred schema: a create executes its CREATE, a refresh must DIFF the new columns against the ones the table already declares and ALTER. That tail is the refresh arm: diff-schema classifies the change and enforces the removal opt-in, and apply-refresh rebuilds the table behind it.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "StringEquals": "refresh" }
          ],
          "Next": "DiffSchema"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "ExecuteDdl"
    },
    "DiffSchema": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Classifies the change into added/removed/retyped/unchanged and enforces the removal opt-in. A removal without allowRemovals is a TERMINAL failure that changes nothing and names the columns in its notification — the refusal IS the warning. This verb also emits the no-change notification itself: the machine has no notification state, and on the unchanged path no Lambda runs after this one.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${diff_schema_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteByChange"
    },
    "RouteByChange": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "An unchanged schema succeeds WITHOUT issuing any DDL — no drop, no create. BOTH outcomes are explicit rules and the Default is a FAILURE, which is the opposite of the mode Choices and deliberately so: those default to the create path because an absent mode has an obvious correct reading, whereas an absent $.diff.changed means diff-schema returned something this machine cannot interpret. Defaulting that to RecordSuccess would hand it to a Pass state that reads $.status — raising the same uncatchable States.Runtime one state later, with the execution stranded and NO recorded outcome. So it records one instead.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "BooleanEquals": true }
          ],
          "Next": "ApplyRefresh"
        },
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "BooleanEquals": false }
          ],
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLock"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "DiffMissingSeed"
    },
    "DiffMissingSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Unreachable while diff-schema always emits $.diff — which is the only reason a seed is cheap enough to be worth having. Same shape as a Task Catch's output so RecordFailure needs no special case. THE ERROR NAME IS DELIBERATELY NOT TerminalError: NO VERB RAISED THIS. diff-schema RETURNED, successfully, just without a $.diff — so @notifies_on_failure never fired and nobody has told the user anything. notify-failure suppresses TerminalError precisely because the decorator already spoke, and wearing that name here would buy this path a Fail state and permanent silence, on a seed whose entire purpose is the shouldn't-happen case. The reason code is unregistered, so the user gets the same generic sentence either way.",
      "Result": {
        "Error": "DiffMissing",
        "Cause": "diff-schema returned no diff"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.error",
      "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
    },
    "ApplyRefresh": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "The refresh apply, replacing the DDL path. ONE glue:UpdateTable, as a read-modify-write on the live table: GetTable, replace the columns and the projection.* parameters, write it back, then re-read to confirm the definition landed. No Athena, no statements, and NOTHING IS EVER DELETED — so there is no window in which the user's table does not exist, and a failed update leaves the old definition standing. That is what makes this Retry safe in a way the destructive pair never was: a re-invocation simply re-reads and writes again. A FRESHNESS GUARD SITS IN FRONT OF THAT WRITE and had to preserve this property explicitly — a Lambda or Glue fault can fire AFTER the update committed, so the retry re-reads a table whose UpdateTime THIS RUN moved, and a guard judging on the stamp alone would fail a run that had already succeeded. It judges on the columns too: a live table already carrying what this run intends to write was written by this run, and the update ahead is an idempotent rewrite. The concurrent-refresh overlap is REDUCED, NOT CLOSED — the Pipe still starts executions FIRE_AND_FORGET, so two refreshes on one database still race, and Glue offers no compare-and-set on UpdateTable; the worst case degrades from 'one run sees no table' to last-write-wins between two definitions.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${apply_refresh_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLock"
    },
    "ExecuteDdl": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Run the single CREATE via Athena (projection ⇒ no MSCK REPAIR), then CONFIRM it. The statement succeeds identically for a run that built the table and a run whose CREATE was a no-op over a table another request built — IF NOT EXISTS cannot tell them apart — so the verb reads the table back and refuses TerminalError table_exists unless the catalog carries this run's own LOCATION, ordered partition keys and per-key projection. Retried here (NOT terminal): a transient Athena/S3 error, a retryable Glue fault on that read-back, and the table not being visible in the catalog yet, since Glue publishes no read-after-write guarantee and failing a create that landed would be worse than the misreport the read-back exists to close; a re-invocation costs a no-op statement and another read. Terminal → Catch → recorded failure: a FAILED/CANCELLED query or poll timeout (ddl_execution_failed / ddl_timeout), a deterministic Glue fault on the read-back (ddl_execution_failed, because a success that cannot be confirmed is not reported as one), and table_exists, which appears in no Retry list because a lost race is an answer rather than a fault.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${execute_ddl_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLock"
    },
    "ReleaseRefreshLock": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Frees the lock on the SUCCESS paths. It sits on a path the create branch shares and is invoked for creates too, which is deliberate: the alternative is a Choice guarding the insertion point, and a Choice whose Variable path is ABSENT raises the uncatchable States.Runtime. Passing the whole state and deciding in Python means no JSONPath is ever evaluated against a key that may not be there. A request with no $.lock is a no-op. ResultPath keeps the running request object intact so RecordSuccess reads the same fields it always did, and the Catch goes STRAIGHT ON rather than to a failure: a release that cannot be written must never turn a successful refresh into a failed one — the lock is recoverable by the staleness steal.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${release_refresh_lock_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.released",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.releaseError",
          "Next": "RecordSuccess"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordSuccess"
    },
    "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "The same verb on the FAILURE paths, so a refusal in diff-schema, a TerminalError in any verb and a caught Lambda fault all release. A separate state rather than a shared one because ASL gives a state exactly one Next, and these two continue to different terminal shaping states. ResultPath is load-bearing here: without it the Task result would REPLACE the state and discard $.error, which RecordFailure reads and which the NotifyFailure decodes — the same mistake the shared-space catches made. Its own Catch continues to RecordFailure for the same reason the success twin's does: the execution has already failed, and a missed release must not change the recorded outcome.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${release_refresh_lock_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.released",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.releaseError",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RecordSuccess": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Shape the terminal SUCCESS outcome the notification producer reads. `status` is now THREADED rather than hard-coded: execute-ddl sets 'created', diff-schema sets 'unchanged' on the no-op path, apply-refresh sets 'refreshed'. Every state that can reach here sets it — which it must, because a Pass state's JSONPath raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime on an absent key, and it would raise it AFTER the work succeeded.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "database.$": "$.space.database",
        "tableName.$": "$.tableName",
        "mode.$": "$.mode",
        "status.$": "$.status"
      },
      "Next": "Succeeded"
    },
    "ValidateFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "A validate-authorize failure REPLACES the state with the error object (its input was the raw SQS array), so its Catch replaced the state with the error object. Seed null identity fields + carry the error under $.error so the shared RecordFailure path is uniform for every verb. `mode` IS SEEDED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON the identity fields are, and it is load-bearing: the failure path added $.mode to RecordFailure so the notification can say create vs refresh, and a Pass state's JSONPath hard-fails on an ABSENT key with an uncatchable States.Runtime — so without this line the ONE failure path that reaches RecordFailure without a validated mode (a malformed or unauthorized request) would die before NotifyFailure and tell the user nothing at all. The literal is 'create' because that is what validate-authorize's own _validate_mode normalises an absent mode to; it is a fallback for wording, never an assertion about what was requested.",
      "Parameters": {
        "traceparent": null,
        "requestId": null,
        "actor": null,
        "space": { "database": null },
        "tableName": null,
        "mode": "create",
        "error.$": "$"
      },
      "Next": "ReleaseRefreshLockOnFailure"
    },
    "RecordFailure": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The single recorded-failure state every verb's Catch reaches. Shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, database, tableName, mode, status:'failed', error } (identity is null on this path: the seed supplies literals, because the error replaced the state) then notifies and fails the execution. It carries the RAW $.error {Error, Cause}, not a decoded reason: the ParseError Pass state that used to produce one ran States.StringToJson, which raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime whenever Cause is absent or is not JSON (States.Timeout, States.DataLimitExceeded, Lambda.Unknown — verified live), killing the execution before it could record anything. Decode with cpcommon.errors.failure_reason, never in ASL. $.mode is read here for the notification and is safe on every path: validate-authorize normalises it onto its output object (so every post-validate Catch preserves it under ResultPath), and ValidateFailureSeed seeds it for the path where the error replaced the state. IT CARRIES THE FORWARDED REQUEST FROM THE CONTEXT OBJECT, $$.Execution.Input, not from the seed that reached it: a failure whose error REPLACED the state has no $.traceparent and no $.requestId left to read, and the envelope is the only surviving copy of the run — notify-failure parses the traceparent and the request id out of it in Python, which is the one place that value is reachable, since prising it out in ASL would need the banned States.StringToJson. Reading $$ here rather than threading it up through the seeds is also what keeps this to one key on one state per plane: the context object cannot be absent, so it is not a read any seed has to supply.",
      "Parameters": {
        "raw.$": "$$.Execution.Input",
        "traceparent.$": "$.traceparent",
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "database.$": "$.space.database",
        "tableName.$": "$.tableName",
        "mode.$": "$.mode",
        "status": "failed",
        "error.$": "$.error"
      },
      "Next": "NotifyFailure"
    },
    "NotifyFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Emit the friendly failure notification for the failures NO VERB CAN CATCH — a cold-start import error, a state timeout, a task that could not be invoked — where @notifies_on_failure never ran and the user would otherwise be told nothing at all. It sits on EVERY failure path rather than a selected one, because the paths where no verb ran are exactly the ones that cannot be selected for in ASL; the verb suppresses the duplicate in Python instead, by reason code. Its Catch goes straight to Failed: a missed notification must never change the recorded outcome, and the execution has already failed.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${notify_failure_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.notified",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.notifyError",
          "Next": "Failed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Failed"
    },
    "Succeeded": {
      "Type": "Succeed"
    },
    "Failed": {
      "Type": "Fail",
      "Error": "TableBuildFailed",
      "Cause": "The table-builder control plane recorded an unrecoverable error (see the RecordFailure state output for the structured { status:'failed', reason } outcome)."
    }
  }
}
